				</a>
			
			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_567049339876413534">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791475792396', '591813437999456887', '569300439979438766', '10463251', '567049339876413534' );" data-tooltip-text="Report this post for inappropriate content.  Reported post are flagged for review by moderators.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Report this post</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_567049339876413534">
				I'm also just curious: why is it OK for programming but not for art? I'm actually a SQL developer (Data Analyst/Engineer), and my job is as much at stake as an artist's — probably even more so. I try to embrace AI as much as possible to avoid being left behind. In my opinion, it's OK to use AI for programming and everything else. Unless you use the environment argument, in which case it's not OK for anything, and I am very much guilty myself. But I don't get why artists always are the big victims in the AI world.			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
									<div></div>
				
				
