						<img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/icon_InLibrary.png" style="" class="inlibrary" title="has Ghost in the SQL Data">																		
						<div data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;row&quot;}" class="community_award_ctn" id="community_awards_comment_562546073917381008"></div>
					</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp">
											</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp" title="October 29, 2026 @ 3:20:29 pm PST" data-timestamp="1793312429">
						Oct 29 @ 3:20pm&nbsp;
					</div>
				</div>

				
				<div class="commentthread_comment_actions" >
														<a class="forum_comment_action forum_comment_action_trigger" data-tooltip-type="selector" data-tooltip-content="#forum_comment_actions_menu_562546073917381008">
					<span><i class="ico16 arrow_down"></i></span>
				</a>
			
			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_562546073917381008">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791475792396', '591813437999456887', '569300439979438766', '30658044', '562546073917381008' );" data-tooltip-text="Report this post for inappropriate content.  Reported post are flagged for review by moderators.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Report this post</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_562546073917381008">
				<blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c562546073917323692">Cilvaa</a></b>:</div><blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c567049339876413534">spitfire_ch</a></b>:</div>I'm also just curious: why is it OK for programming but not for art?</blockquote><br>Art is by nature creative. Coding, like any language, has rules. While yes you could write several versions of code in the same language, and achieve the same result; ultimately it all falls back on structured rules. <br><br>If I ask 100 coders to build a basic web UI based on a layout sketch and a list of requirements, they might lay things out very differently, use different CSS properties for styling, use different colours and fonts, different CSS templates; but core components will be coded nearly identically. Certain systems need to be written a specific way just to function.<br><br>If I ask an AI to create an API that can read data from a SharePoint list, it doesn't need to &quot;create&quot; it, there is documentation on the Microsoft website it can follow to write it. If you asked a human to do it, and they didn't already know how, they'll read the same documentation and arrive at a near-identical result. That's not very creative of the AI, that's just following the instructions. And it doesn't need to steal ideas from programmers, documentation for languages and things like APIs are very accessible. If an AI doesn't know how to do something, it can literally just read the manual.<br><br>If I ask 100 artists to &quot;draw a cat sitting at a window&quot;, I will get 100 quite different drawings. That is truely creative because that is a vague set of parameters and they can use any mediums they want, paper or digital, pencil or pen, black and white or colour, realistic or cartoony, viewing the cat from inside the window or outside, with perspective or orthographic, etc. </blockquote><br>I think several coders would not create totally different code many many times. If you ask them to write a function, yes, they will be similar, but if you ask them to code the same application they will think different architectures with different components, so they will need different code in them. There are a ton of ways to solve the same problem.<br><br>The main difference between art and coding in the AI subject is that nobody can do all the code of a program without the design of what they want and some insight about how to create a program. They still hire coders to code with AI. But with art and translations they don't hire artists, I think. Also images are created by copying images while language is different, AI can create text of its own, the original texts or programs used in training are much more diluted and it can create texts that never existed before.			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
									<div></div>
				
				
									<div class="forum_comment_permlink">
						<span class="forum_comment_moderate">
													</span>
												<a data-panel="{&quot;focusable&quot;:false}" href="#c562546073917381008">#9</a>
											</div>
							</div>
		</div>
	</div>
		
	
	<div data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;row&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;PanelGroup&quot;}" class="commentthread_comment responsive_body_text   " id="comment_562546073917426117" style="">

