					</div>
				</div>

				
				<div class="commentthread_comment_actions" >
														<a class="forum_comment_action forum_comment_action_trigger" data-tooltip-type="selector" data-tooltip-content="#forum_comment_actions_menu_562546398187386988">
					<span><i class="ico16 arrow_down"></i></span>
				</a>
			
			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_562546398187386988">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791475792396', '591813437999456887', '569300439979438766', '15634883', '562546398187386988' );" data-tooltip-text="Report this post for inappropriate content.  Reported post are flagged for review by moderators.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.fastly.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Report this post</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_562546398187386988">
				<blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c562546073917323692">Cilvaa</a></b>:</div><blockquote class="bb_blockquote with_author"><div class="bb_quoteauthor">Originally posted by <b><a href="#c567049339876413534">spitfire_ch</a></b>:</div>I'm also just curious: why is it OK for programming but not for art?</blockquote><br>Art is by nature creative. Coding, like any language, has rules. While yes you could write several versions of code in the same language, and achieve the same result; ultimately it all falls back on structured rules. <br><br>If I ask 100 coders to build a basic web UI based on a layout sketch and a list of requirements, they might lay things out very differently, use different CSS properties for styling, use different colours and fonts, different CSS templates; but core components will be coded nearly identically. Certain systems need to be written a specific way just to function.<br><br>If I ask an AI to create an API that can read data from a SharePoint list, it doesn't need to &quot;create&quot; it, there is documentation on the Microsoft website it can follow to write it. If you asked a human to do it, and they didn't already know how, they'll read the same documentation and arrive at a near-identical result. That's not very creative of the AI, that's just following the instructions. And it doesn't need to steal ideas from programmers, documentation for languages and things like APIs are very accessible. If an AI doesn't know how to do something, it can literally just read the manual.<br><br>If I ask 100 artists to &quot;draw a cat sitting at a window&quot;, I will get 100 quite different drawings. That is truely creative because that is a vague set of parameters and they can use any mediums they want, paper or digital, pencil or pen, black and white or colour, realistic or cartoony, viewing the cat from inside the window or outside, with perspective or orthographic, etc. </blockquote><br>What a dumb take...  Sure there will be some cross-over with libraries, and maybe some basic functions... but if you think every coder will return the exact same product in the end, that's horrible incorrect.  Most websites look the same because the internet is stagnating and people keep jumping to the same frameworks to handle the styling, but the code behind the scenes actually doing the stuff is likely quite different between sites.<br><br>If we go back to the early 00s when the internet still had personality, sites were wildly different from eachother.			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
									<div></div>
				
				
									<div class="forum_comment_permlink">
						<span class="forum_comment_moderate">
													</span>
